## 137: two independent right-angle motion models

The word "speed" in these constant-speed textbook motion problems is used
as the distance/time rate; there is no acceleration data to infer a
time-varying law. Preserve all original distances, times and directions.

a) Let u be northbound speed and v eastbound speed, in km/h, both positive.
The distances after 4 hours are 4u and 4v, perpendicular. Thus
16(u^2+v^2)=24^2, and 4(u-v)=4.8; equivalently
u^2+v^2=36, u-v=1.2.
Substitution gives v^2+1.2v-17.28=0=(v-3.6)(v+4.8).
Positivity excludes v=-4.8. Therefore v=3.6 and u=4.8.
Check distances: 19.2 and 14.4 km; difference 4.8;
19.2^2+14.4^2=576=24^2.

b) Let u and v be the first and second speeds, in m/s, both positive.
Equal distances give 6u=8v, hence u:v=4:3. After 15 s,
225(u^2+v^2)=9, so sqrt(u^2+v^2)=0.2.
Write u=4k,v=3k with k>0: 5k=0.2, k=0.04.
Thus u=0.16, v=0.12. Distances after 15 s are 2.4 and 1.8 m;
their squared sum is 9. Also 6(0.16)=8(0.12)=0.96 m.

Tasks a and b have independent data and distinct rate constraints (difference
versus a ratio); solving one does not settle the other. Use two complete-pair
single questions, never one click per unknown. Selection does not assess
unaided equation formulation, positive-root selection or written derivation.

